Rem ==================================================================
Rem lin.shoddy â€” test program for machines/lin.shoddy
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the csvtest.shoddy / fin.shoddy / eng.shoddy
Rem pattern: it prints what it is doing, stops at the first failed
Rem assertion, and ends with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when none fail.
Rem
Rem It includes BOTH matrix.shoddy and lin.shoddy, because that is what a
Rem program using lin has to do: matrix owns the type and lin owns the
Rem algorithms, and since v1.6.0 lin's own includes do not bleed through
Rem to a consumer. Eng* words are not in scope here for the same reason,
Rem so SumOf and Close below are this file's own.
Rem
Rem Two styles of check, and both are needed:
Rem
Rem   * KNOWN ANSWERS, worked by hand. LinDet of {{1,2},{3,4}} is -2.
Rem     The eigenvalues of the 2,-1 tridiagonal are 2 - sqrt 2, 2 and
Rem     2 + sqrt 2. These catch a word that is confidently wrong.
Rem   * RESIDUALS against the defining identity - P A - L U, Q R - A,
Rem     L L' - A, A x - b, A v - lambda v. These catch a word that is
Rem     wrong in a way no single number would show, and they are why the
Rem     factorisations are testable at all: there is more than one valid
Rem     L and U, but only one valid product.
Rem
Rem What cannot be tested here: the aborting half of every guard. Shoddy
Rem has no catchable errors, so LinDet's refusal on a non-square matrix,
Rem LinInv's and LinSolve's on a singular one, LinChol's on a matrix that
Rem is not positive definite, LinQrOf's on dependent columns and
Rem LinCross's on anything but three dimensions are verified by hand and
Rem named here instead.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/matrix.shoddy"
Include "../machines/lin.shoddy"

Def Close(a As Number, b As Number, tol As Number) As Boolean
    Abs(a - b) <= tol

Def SumOf(xs As List Of Number) As Number
    Fold(xs, 0, Fn(s, x) => s + x)

Rem The distance between two matrices, which is how every factorisation
Rem below is checked: rebuild the original from its parts and measure how
Rem far the rebuild lands from where it started.
Def Apart(a As Matrix, b As Matrix) As Number
    LinNorm(MatSub(a, b))

Def Main()
    Rem A is the smallest matrix with nothing special about it: not
    Rem symmetric, not triangular, not singular, and its pivot is not on
    Rem the diagonal, so partial pivoting has to do something.
    Let a = Mat(2, 2, { 1, 2, 3, 4 })
    Rem B is the 2,-1 tridiagonal - symmetric, positive definite, and with
    Rem eigenvalues that are known in closed form.
    Let b = Mat(3, 3, { 2, 0 - 1, 0, 0 - 1, 2, 0 - 1, 0, 0 - 1, 2 })
    Rem S is singular: its second row is twice its first.
    Let s = Mat(3, 3, { 1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 6, 1, 1, 1 })

    Print("--- SHAPE AND MEASURE ---")
    Assert(LinIsSquare(a), "A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Assert(LinIsSquare(Mat(2, 3, { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 })) = False, "AND ONE THAT IS NOT")
    Assert(Close(LinTrace(a), 5, 0.0000000001), "TRACE")
    Assert(Close(LinTrace(b), 6, 0.0000000001), "TRACE OF THE TRIDIAGONAL")
    Assert(Close(LinNorm(Ident(3)), 1.7320508076, 0.0000000001),
           "THE FROBENIUS NORM OF AN IDENTITY IS ROOT N")
    Assert(Close(LinMaxAbs(Mat(2, 2, { 1, 0 - 5, 3, 2 })), 5, 0.0000000001),
           "LARGEST MAGNITUDE, SIGN IGNORED")
    Assert(LinIsSym(b, 0.000000001), "THE TRIDIAGONAL IS SYMMETRIC")
    Assert(LinIsSym(a, 0.000000001) = False, "AND A IS NOT")

    Print("--- VECTORS ---")
    Assert(Close(LinVNorm(MatRow(Mat(1, 3, { 3, 4, 0 }), 1)), 5, 0.0000000001),
           "VECTOR LENGTH")
    Assert(Close(LinVNorm(LinVUnit(MatRow(Mat(1, 3, { 3, 4, 0 }), 1))), 1,
                 0.0000000001), "A UNIT VECTOR HAS LENGTH ONE")
    Rem The columns of the identity are the basis vectors, and e1 x e2 = e3
    Rem is the right-hand rule.
    Assert(Close(LinVNorm(VSub(LinCross(MatCol(Ident(3), 1), MatCol(Ident(3), 2)),
                               MatCol(Ident(3), 3))), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "THE CROSS PRODUCT FOLLOWS THE RIGHT-HAND RULE")
    Rem And it anticommutes: e2 x e1 is -e3.
    Assert(Close(LinVNorm(VAdd(LinCross(MatCol(Ident(3), 2), MatCol(Ident(3), 1)),
                               MatCol(Ident(3), 3))), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND REVERSING THE ARGUMENTS REVERSES IT")
    Assert(Close(Dot(LinCross(MatRow(Mat(1, 3, { 1, 2, 3 }), 1),
                              MatRow(Mat(1, 3, { 4, 5, 6 }), 1)),
                     MatRow(Mat(1, 3, { 1, 2, 3 }), 1)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND IS PERPENDICULAR TO WHAT MADE IT")

    Print("--- ECHELON FORMS AND RANK ---")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LinRef(a), 2, 1), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "ROW ECHELON FORM IS ZERO BELOW THE PIVOT")
    Assert(Close(Apart(LinRref(a), Ident(2)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND A NONSINGULAR MATRIX REDUCES TO THE IDENTITY")
    Assert(LinRank(Ident(3)) = 3, "AN IDENTITY HAS FULL RANK")
    Rem S has a row that is twice another, so one of the three is free.
    Assert(LinRank(s) = 2, "AND A DEPENDENT ROW COSTS A RANK")
    Assert(LinRank(Mat(2, 3, { 1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 6 })) = 1,
           "RANK WORKS ON A MATRIX THAT IS NOT SQUARE")
    Assert(LinRank(MatFill(2, 2, 0)) = 0, "AND A ZERO MATRIX HAS NO RANK AT ALL")
    Assert(Cols(LinAug(a, Ident(2))) = 4, "AUGMENTING WIDENS")
    Assert(Rows(LinAug(a, Ident(2))) = 2, "AND DOES NOT LENGTHEN")

    Print("--- DETERMINANT AND INVERSE ---")
    Assert(Close(LinDet(a), 0 - 2, 0.0000000001), "DETERMINANT")
    Assert(Close(LinDet(b), 4, 0.0000000001), "DETERMINANT OF THE TRIDIAGONAL")
    Assert(Close(LinDet(Ident(4)), 1, 0.0000000001), "AN IDENTITY HAS DETERMINANT ONE")
    Rem The row that is twice another makes this exactly zero, and the
    Rem pivot search is what has to notice.
    Assert(Close(LinDet(s), 0, 0.0000000001), "AND A SINGULAR MATRIX HAS NONE")
    Rem Two rows swapped flips the sign, which is the property the sign
    Rem tracking in the elimination exists to preserve.
    Assert(Close(LinDet(LinSwapRows(a, 1, 2)), 2, 0.0000000001),
           "A ROW SWAP FLIPS THE SIGN")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LinInv(a), 1, 1), 0 - 2, 0.0000000001), "INVERSE, BY HAND")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LinInv(a), 2, 1), 1.5, 0.0000000001), "AND THE REST OF IT")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(a, LinInv(a)), Ident(2)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND A MATRIX TIMES ITS INVERSE IS THE IDENTITY")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(b, LinInv(b)), Ident(3)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AT THREE BY THREE TOO")
    Assert(Close(LinCofactor(b, 1, 1), 3, 0.0000000001), "A COFACTOR")
    Assert(Rows(LinMinor(b, 1, 1)) = 2, "A MINOR IS ONE SMALLER")
    Rem A times its adjugate is the determinant times the identity - the
    Rem identity that makes the adjugate worth having.
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(a, LinAdj(a)), MatScale(Ident(2), LinDet(a))), 0,
                 0.0000000001), "AND A TIMES ITS ADJUGATE IS DET TIMES I")

    Print("--- LINEAR SYSTEMS ---")
    Rem x + 2y = 5, 3x + 4y = 11, so x = 1 and y = 2.
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinSolve(a, MatRow(Mat(1, 2, { 5, 11 }), 1)), 1), 1,
                 0.0000000001), "THE FIRST UNKNOWN")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinSolve(a, MatRow(Mat(1, 2, { 5, 11 }), 1)), 2), 2,
                 0.0000000001), "AND THE SECOND")
    Rem The residual, which needs no hand-worked answer and would catch a
    Rem solution that satisfied only some of the equations.
    Assert(Close(LinVNorm(VSub(MatVec(b, LinSolve(b, MatRow(Mat(1, 3, { 1, 0, 0 }), 1))),
                               MatRow(Mat(1, 3, { 1, 0, 0 }), 1))), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND THE RESIDUAL OF A THREE BY THREE SOLVE IS ZERO")

    Print("--- MATRIX POWERS ---")
    Assert(Close(Apart(LinPow(a, 0), Ident(2)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "THE ZEROTH POWER IS THE IDENTITY")
    Assert(Close(Apart(LinPow(a, 1), a), 0, 0.0000000001), "THE FIRST IS ITSELF")
    Rem A^3 = {{37, 54}, {81, 118}}, worked by hand from A^2 = {{7,10},{15,22}}.
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LinPow(a, 3), 1, 1), 37, 0.0000000001), "THE CUBE")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LinPow(a, 3), 2, 2), 118, 0.0000000001), "AND ITS FAR CORNER")
    Assert(Close(Apart(LinPow(a, 3), MatMul(MatMul(a, a), a)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND SQUARE-AND-MULTIPLY AGREES WITH THE LONG WAY")
    Rem A negative power inverts first.
    Assert(Close(Apart(LinPow(a, 0 - 1), LinInv(a)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "THE MINUS FIRST POWER IS THE INVERSE")

    Print("--- LU FACTORISATION ---")
    Rem P A = L U is the whole claim, and only the product is unique.
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(Perm(LinLuOf(a)), a),
                       MatMul(LFactor(LinLuOf(a)), UFactor(LinLuOf(a)))), 0,
                 0.0000000001), "P A IS L U")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(Perm(LinLuOf(b)), b),
                       MatMul(LFactor(LinLuOf(b)), UFactor(LinLuOf(b)))), 0,
                 0.0000000001), "AT THREE BY THREE TOO")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LFactor(LinLuOf(a)), 1, 1), 1, 0.0000000001),
           "DOOLITTLE PUTS THE UNIT DIAGONAL ON L")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LFactor(LinLuOf(a)), 2, 2), 1, 0.0000000001), "ALL OF IT")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LFactor(LinLuOf(a)), 1, 2), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND L IS LOWER TRIANGULAR")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(UFactor(LinLuOf(a)), 2, 1), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND U IS UPPER TRIANGULAR")
    Rem A's pivot is in the second row, so the permutation is a real swap
    Rem and its sign is -1. This is the part a test on a tidy matrix misses.
    Assert(Close(PSign(LinLuOf(a)), 0 - 1, 0.0000000001), "A SWAP COSTS A SIGN")
    Rem And the sign is exactly what makes the determinant come back out.
    Assert(Close(PSign(LinLuOf(a)) * MatGet(UFactor(LinLuOf(a)), 1, 1)
                 * MatGet(UFactor(LinLuOf(a)), 2, 2), LinDet(a), 0.0000000001),
           "AND THE PIVOTS TIMES THE SIGN ARE THE DETERMINANT")

    Print("--- QR FACTORISATION ---")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(QFactor(LinQrOf(a)), RFactor(LinQrOf(a))), a), 0,
                 0.0000000001), "Q R IS A")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(Transp(QFactor(LinQrOf(a))), QFactor(LinQrOf(a))),
                       Ident(2)), 0, 0.0000000001), "AND Q IS ORTHONORMAL")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(RFactor(LinQrOf(a)), 2, 1), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND R IS UPPER TRIANGULAR")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(QFactor(LinQrOf(b)), RFactor(LinQrOf(b))), b), 0,
                 0.0000000001), "AT THREE BY THREE TOO")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(Transp(QFactor(LinQrOf(b))), QFactor(LinQrOf(b))),
                       Ident(3)), 0, 0.0000000001), "AND ORTHONORMAL THERE TOO")

    Print("--- CHOLESKY ---")
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(LinChol(b), Transp(LinChol(b))), b), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "L TIMES L TRANSPOSE IS A")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LinChol(b), 1, 1), 1.4142135624, 0.0000000001),
           "AND THE FIRST PIVOT IS ROOT TWO")
    Assert(Close(MatGet(LinChol(b), 1, 2), 0, 0.0000000001), "AND L IS LOWER TRIANGULAR")
    Assert(Close(Apart(LinChol(Ident(3)), Ident(3)), 0, 0.0000000001),
           "AND AN IDENTITY FACTORS TO ITSELF")

    Print("--- CHARACTERISTIC POLYNOMIAL ---")
    Rem A has char poly x^2 - 5x - 2: the trace with its sign flipped,
    Rem then the determinant.
    Assert(Length(LinCharPoly(a)) = 3, "A TWO BY TWO HAS THREE COEFFICIENTS")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinCharPoly(a), 1), 1, 0.0000000001), "MONIC, HIGHEST POWER FIRST")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinCharPoly(a), 2), 0 - 5, 0.0000000001),
           "AND THE SECOND COEFFICIENT IS MINUS THE TRACE")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinCharPoly(a), 3), 0 - 2, 0.0000000001),
           "AND THE LAST IS THE DETERMINANT")
    Rem B has char poly x^3 - 6x^2 + 10x - 4.
    Assert(Length(LinCharPoly(b)) = 4, "A THREE BY THREE HAS FOUR")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinCharPoly(b), 2), 0 - 6, 0.0000000001), "TRIDIAGONAL TRACE TERM")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinCharPoly(b), 3), 10, 0.0000000001), "MIDDLE TERM")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinCharPoly(b), 4), 0 - 4, 0.0000000001), "AND CONSTANT TERM")

    Print("--- EIGENVALUES ---")
    Rem The 2,-1 tridiagonal has eigenvalues 2 - sqrt 2, 2, 2 + sqrt 2.
    Rem The scan runs upward, so they come back in order.
    Assert(Length(LinEigVals(b)) = 3, "THREE REAL EIGENVALUES")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinEigVals(b), 1), 0.5857864376, 0.0000001), "THE SMALLEST")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinEigVals(b), 2), 2, 0.0000001), "THE MIDDLE ONE")
    Assert(Close(Nth(LinEigVals(b), 3), 3.4142135624, 0.0000001), "AND THE LARGEST")
    Assert(Close(SumOf(LinEigVals(b)), LinTrace(b), 0.0000001),
           "AND THEY SUM TO THE TRACE")
    Rem A rotation by 90 degrees has no real eigenvalue at all - nothing it
    Rem acts on keeps its direction. The word finds none and says so by
    Rem returning fewer than Rows(m), which is the documented signal.
    Assert(Length(LinEigVals(Mat(2, 2, { 0, 0 - 1, 1, 0 }))) = 0,
           "A ROTATION HAS NO REAL EIGENVALUE")

    Print("--- THE SYMMETRIC EIGENPROBLEM ---")
    Assert(Length(Values(LinEigSym(b))) = 3, "JACOBI RETURNS A FULL SET")
    Assert(Close(SumOf(Values(LinEigSym(b))), 6, 0.0000001), "SUMMING TO THE TRACE")
    Rem Orthonormal eigenvectors, which is what symmetry buys.
    Assert(Close(Apart(MatMul(Transp(Vectors(LinEigSym(b))), Vectors(LinEigSym(b))),
                       Ident(3)), 0, 0.0000001), "WITH ORTHONORMAL VECTORS")
    Rem And the defining property, checked pairwise: A v = lambda v for the
    Rem value and the column that share an index.
    Assert(Close(LinVNorm(VSub(MatVec(b, MatCol(Vectors(LinEigSym(b)), 1)),
                               VScale(MatCol(Vectors(LinEigSym(b)), 1),
                                      Nth(Values(LinEigSym(b)), 1)))), 0, 0.0000001),
           "A V IS LAMBDA V FOR THE FIRST PAIR")
    Assert(Close(LinVNorm(VSub(MatVec(b, MatCol(Vectors(LinEigSym(b)), 3)),
                               VScale(MatCol(Vectors(LinEigSym(b)), 3),
                                      Nth(Values(LinEigSym(b)), 3)))), 0, 0.0000001),
           "AND FOR THE THIRD")
    Rem An identity is already diagonal, so the sweep must do nothing and
    Rem must not divide by the zero it finds off the diagonal.
    Assert(Close(SumOf(Values(LinEigSym(Ident(3)))), 3, 0.0000001),
           "AND AN IDENTITY IS ALL ONES")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")
